ABSTRACT. In this communication, we report an efficient and flexible perovskite solar cell based on formamidinium lead trihalide (FAPbI3) with simplified configuration. The device achieved a champion efficiency of $12.70\%$ , utilizing direct contact between metallic indium-tinoxide (ITO) electrode and perovskite absorber. The underlying working mechanism is proposed subsequently, via a systematic investigation focusing on the heterojunction within this device. A significant charge storage has been observed in the perovskite, which is believed to generate photovoltage and serves as the driving force for charge transferring from the absorber to ITO electrode as well. More importantly, this simplified device structure on flexible substrates suggests its compatibility for scale-up fabrication, which paves the way for commercialization of perovskite photovoltaic technology.
Keywords:  perovskite, solar cell, heterojunction, built-in potential, working mechanism
## Nano Letters
Inorganic/organic hybrid metal halide perovskites, enabling the assembly of organic and inorganic components within a single molecular-scale, represents a family of unique semiconductors featured with compositional versatility and superior optoelectronic properties such as excellent defects tolerance, suitable band structure, ambipolar carrier transport and long carrier lifetime1,2,3,4. They have been found to have promising applications in high-performance optoelectronics, especially as photovoltaic cells5,6,7. The power conversion efficiency (PCE) has rapidly increased to over $20\%$ in perovskite solar cells8 following the initial report in $2009^{9}$ . Now that perovskite technology has been regarded as a serious player in the photovoltaic field, it has the potential to compete with crystalline silicon solar cells.
In planar type perovskite solar cells, the devices adopt layer-to-layer configuration, where the perovskite absorber layer is sandwiched by both electron/hole transport layers (ETL/HTL)10,11. Upon irradiation, carriers are generated in the perovskite layer, extracted by the adjacent ETL and HTL and eventually collected by electrodes. Metal oxide materials such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ are widely used as ETL in perovskite solar cells for their mature application in the photovoltaic field2,12,13. The $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, however, requires a high temperature sintering process to obtain intimate contact, whereas for manufacturing purposes it is desirable to obtain low temperature processes14. Even a compact layer of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with a thickness of tens of nanometers in planar perovskite solar cells needs sub- $150\ \mathrm{{^\circC}}$ process15,16, which also precludes the use of plastic PET substrates. In order to translate lab-scale device fabrication to a fab-scale commercial production it is preferred to obtain a proper selective layer which requires a low temperature process-for example, the solar cell can be constructed on flexible polymer-based substrates. Until now, a series of laudable attempts to pursue high-performance perovskite solar cells with low temperature process based on organic charge transport materials17,18,19, and inorganic semiconductors like $Z_{\mathrm{nO}}^{20,21}$ have been reported. As one step forward, a more simplified device architecture is believed to be more important for reducing the issues due to mass process in device fabrication. Recently, it has also been documented that decent PCEs are able to be achieved in devices without either HTL22,23 or ETL24,25. Despite of the practical demonstrations, the details regarding the working mechanisms in such a device remain unclear26,27, for example how carriers behave within the perovskite thin film and further build up the photovoltage in such devices.  Thus, it becomes desirable to gain further insight into the transport layer-free devices.
In this work, we systematically explored a simplified device without any electron transport layer. Firstly, a formamidinium based perovskite film deposited directly onto a tin-indium-oxide (ITO) substrate was used as the photoactive layer due to its narrowed bandgap for spectral match with sun light. A champion PCE of $12.70\%$ was achieved for the flexible perovskite solar cell based on ITO/PET $(\mathrm{ITO}_{\mathrm{(PET)}})$ substrate, highlighting the great potential of the device design and fabrication process for future roll-to-roll manufacturing. More importantly, photogenerated charge behavior has been carefully probed to illustrate working mechanism of the device by using Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM), X-ray diffraction (XRD), ultraviolet photoelectron spectrum (UPS) and capacitance-voltage (C-V) analysis. Our results show a negligible built-in potential was formed across the perovskite/ITO heterojunction, meaning that the built-in potential in this heterojunction is not essential for electron extraction. Moreover, a large capacitance in such device is measured, which could ensure the proper photovoltage and provide concentration carrier concentration gradient that drives charge transfer from perovskite to ITO. Though p-i-n type device structure still dominates the field, we argue alternative device design without electron/hole selective contact is promising according to our previous success in HTL free devices14,28.  Meanwhile, our findings also indicate the potential opportunity for applying various highly conductive materials for perovskite device development.
Results and discussion. The organic cation of the inorganic/organic hybrid perovskite can be used to tune the optical bandgap which provides great versatility in crystal structure and composition 29-32. For example, perovskite with a $\mathrm{CH(NH_{2})_{2}}^{+}$ cation, which is larger than $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ , shows a smaller optical bandgap with promising more efficient infrared light harvesting and corresponding high photocurrent33,34. In this work, the hybrid inorganic/organic perovskite was synthesized using $\mathrm{CH(NH}_{2}\mathrm{)}_{2}^{+}$ , while $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ was used as an additive to provide good film quality with enhanced interfacial opto-electric property via chlorine incorporation3,35,36. A two-step solution process was adapted here to synthesize chlorine incorporated $\mathrm{CH(NH_{2})_{2}P b I_{3}}$ (Cl-FAPbI3) as illustrated in Fig. 1a ( see details in methods section). The perovskite films were directly formed on ITO substrate without any charge selective layers. Before spin-coating $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ layer, the flexible ITO substrate was treated in UVozone for 30 mins. Typically UV-ozone treatment increases the work function of ITO film due to the oxidation of the surface and also removes organic residues and enhances surface wetting37. Fig. 1b provides a top view SEM image of the morphology of the synthesized Cl-FAPbI3 film on ITO(PET) substrate. it shows the clear formation of a uniform, compact Cl-FAPbI3 layer. We further confirm the Cl-FAPbI3 crystalline structure using XRD. It is worth emphasizing that the peak at $13^{\mathrm{{o}}}$ in XRD indicates residual $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in the as-synthesized Cl-FAPbI3 compound as discussed later. Contrary to the previous results6,34, the sharp diffraction peaks shown in Fig. 1c indicate the high crystallinity of Cl-FAPbI3 samples38 with $120^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ annealing procedure, allowing us to further explore its intrinsic physical property and photoelectric properties. A additional XRD profile of $\operatorname{Cl-FAPbI}_{3}$ stored in air ( $48\%$ humidity) with the peak of $11.5^{\mathrm{{\circ}}}$ indicate a new crystalline phase as shown in Fig. S1, which is assumed to originate from $\mathrm{(FA)_{4}P b I_{6}}{\bullet2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ due to Cl-FAPbI3 hydration39.
A flexible device with Cl-FAPbI3 deposited directly on ITO(PET) (denoted as device A) is presented. The corresponding photocurrent density-voltage (J-V) characteristics of devices under AM 1.5G illumination is shown in Fig. 2a. The applied voltage scan rate in J-V measurement was kept at $20~\mathrm{mV}$ per step with a delay time of 0.001 s. A champion PCE of $12.70\%$ for a reverse scan with a short circuit current density $(\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of $20.70\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , an open circuit voltage $(\mathrm{V_{OC}})$ of $0.955^{}\mathrm{~V~}$ , and a fill factor (FF) of 0.64, represent an efficient flexible perovskite photovoltaic cell. It is interesting to note that in the J-V curve there is some undesired oscillation, which will be discussed later.  However, the stabilized power output current density at $0.7\mathrm{V}$ is presented in fig. S2 with stable output PCE of $6.8\%$ . Thus we believe this efficient simplified device without hole blocking layer (HBL) holds huge potential toward the roll-to-roll fabrication. However, devices without $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ as an additive in the active layer fabrication process had poor performance as shown in Fig. S3 and this device got rapidly bleached in air. In our previous work, solid evidence has shown that chlorine incorporation can affect not only the formation of perovskite films, but also the relevant interface, which results in improved optoelectronic characteristics due to enhanced charge transferring across the heterojunction interface rather than within perovskite crystals40. The incident photon-to-current conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectrum for device A is shown in Fig. 2b. It shows a response from $305\mathrm{nm}$ to $835\mathrm{nm}$ , which is in good agreement with the optical absorption (presented in Fig. S4). The relatively flat spectral profile approaching $80\%$ is indicative of excellent light harvesting for high photocurrent41. For better performance evaluation of this hole-blocking layer-free flexible device A, Fig. 2c gives a histogram showing PCE distribution of those devices in one batch. An average PCE of $11.5\%$ was achieved with half of the devices having bigger PCE than $11\%$ . The narrow PCE distribution strongly indicates the good reproducibility of our device processing.
In order to determine the robustness in mechanical properties of the flexible devices, a bending test was carried out. After bending 20 times, the J-V characteristics of device A are shown in Fig. 2a in comparison with the pristine device, and the key photovoltaic parameters are tabulated in Table $1.\mathrm{V_{OC}}$ and Jsc slightly decreased after bending, and the major PCE loss is also contributed from the degradation in FF. Considering the robustness of each component in this device, the degradation of FF should be ascribed to the increasing resistance in Au counter electrode since small cracks can are visible by the human eye after bending.
To further understand the working mechanism of this HBL free device two different devices with/without $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ were fabricated on an ITO/glass substrate $\mathrm{(ITO_{\mathrm{(glass)}})}$ . Devices B and C consist of ITO(glass)/Cl-FAPbI3/spiro-OMeTAD/Au, $\mathrm{ITO}_{(\mathrm{glass})}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cl}$ -FAPbI3/spiro-OMeTAD/Au, respectively. Cross-sectional SEM images of the two cell configurations are presented in Fig. S5. The J-V characteristics of the devices under AM1.5G 1 sun illumination are given in Fig. 3a. The detailed photovoltaic parameters, i.e., VOC, FF, JSC and PCE for devices B and C are tabulated in Table 2.  A PCE of $15.45\%$ was achieved in device B without HBL, comparable to that of $15.32\%$ in reference cell, device C, with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as HBL. Unfortunately, the undesired oscillation also appears in J-V curves for for device B. For each point in the I-V curve, the product of the current and voltage represents the output power for that operating condition. The measurement of photocurrent in each applied voltage should be performed after a delay time until the test cell is in an equilibrium condition, which depends on intrinsic properties of the device. Generally, the delay time is 4 times as long as the time constant, which is time for reaching (1-1/e) of photocurrent response to the applied bias42. As shown in Fig. S6, it is J-V curves as illustration for devices containing ITO(PET)/Cl-FAPbI3, ITO(glass)/Cl-FAPbI3 and TiO2/Cl-FAPbI3 heterojunction, respectively. The large hysteresis window in devices containing ITO/Cl-FAPbI3 (Fig. S6a and S6b) with different scanning rate and different scanning direction indicates an unbalance between electrons and holes transport in the device6. Contrarily, a negligible hysteresis and oscillation is observed in J-V curve of device with the TiO2/Cl-FAPbI3 heterojunction (Fig. S6c). Thus, it is reasonable to ascribe the oscillation to incongruous transport between photogenerated electrons and holes in the device. To further clarify the impact of chlorine on intrinsic characteristic of photoactive layer, Fig.S7a and Fig. S7b shows the topview SEM of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ and Cl-FAPbI3 films with the same fabrication process as devices, respectively.   However, no great difference in morphology is observed. It is worth to note that FAPbI3 film showed a worse stability in air. And XRD profile showed obvious FAI residue in film as shown in Fig. S7c indicating MACl had significant effect on FAPbI3 formation as that to $\mathrm{MAPbI_{3}}^{40}$ and the hydrophilic residue FAI made contribution to hydration.
In typical perovskite solar cells, charge carrier lifetimes within the charge selective layers play a vital role in the corresponding device performance since the carrier lifetime in the photoactive layer (perovskite) is large enough to reach the selective interface43. These selective materials in the device also act as the counter charge blocking layer to yield high charge collection efficiency. Thus, the collected charge lifetime in the hole-blocking layer-free devices should be short as the photogenerated holes in perovskite can come in direct contact with the electrons in the ITO layer (device A and B) since ITO is the conductor for both electron and hole. Here, we carried out transient photovoltage decay measurements to estimate the interfacial charge recombination process in devices. The obtained photovoltage decay of device B and C as a function of time is shown in Fig. 3b. As expected, a smaller time constant being $13~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ is gathered from the exponential fitting for device B. This result indicates the interfacial recombination in device B is more awful comparing to that in device C with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ acting as hole-blocking layer, whose time constant in interfacial recombination is $90~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ .
A general single diode model was used, according to Site’s method44, to evaluate the photogenerated charge transporting properties of flexible devices under AM1.5G illumination. Fig. 3c shows the statistics of fitting results of series resistance $(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{S}})$ . The average $\mathtt{R}_{\mathtt{S}}$ in device B is about $45\ \Omega/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , larger than that $(\sim32~\Omega/\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ in device $\mathrm{~C~}$ with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact layer on rigid ITO/glass. This result goes against our expectation since ITO is more conductive than semiconductor $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer. To clarify the reason for enlarging the $\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{S}}$ , the $\mathtt{R}_{\mathtt{S}}$ statistics in devices in dark with different spin coating rate for FAI is presented in Fig. S8. The results indicate that the large $\mathtt{R}_{\mathtt{S}}$ is not caused by excess FAI or ITO corroded by FAI. Here, the results obtained from photovoltage decay and $\mathtt{R}_{\mathtt{S}}$ indicates perovskite/ITO heterojunction has negative influence on carriers (electrons) transport. However, it is interesting to see the device based on such a heterojunction still give rise to photovoltaic performance.
KPFM was then employed to investigate the Cl-FAPbI3/ITO heterojunction. Fig. 4 shows the topography and potential spatial maps of ITO and Cl-FAPbI3. A small roughness of $1.4\:\mathrm{nm}$ in the ITO film can be obtained from height image in Fig. 4a. According to Fig. 4c, the roughness of Cl-FAPbI3 film is $19.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ , indicating a comparable uniform and compact layer loaded on ITO. The smoothness of the layer could be an important factor that enables efficient photogenerated charge dissociation/transport in perovskite solar cells. As shown in Fig. 5b and 5d, the twodimensional image overlaying the surface potential spatial on the topographical image of ClFAPbI3 shows an apparent difference between the grain boundaries and the bulk. Moreover, the relative values in surface work function of ITO and $\mathrm{Cl-FAPbI}_{3}$ can also be determined by the contact potential difference $\left(\mathrm{CPD}\right)^{47}$ :
$$
V_{\mathit{C P D}}\ =\ ^{(\phi_{t i p}\ -\phi_{s a m p l e})}\bigg/\%
$$
Where $\pmb{\phi}_{s a m p l e}$ and $\phi_{t i p}$ are the work functions of the sample and tips, and e is the element charge. Using the same scanning tip during the measurement, $\varPhi_{t i p}$ can be treated as a constant value. Referring to inserted potential line in Fig. 5b and 5d, the CPD of both ITO and Cl-FAPbI3 are ${\sim}1.2\mathrm{~V~}$ . The difference of energy level in two materials is insufficient to form a built-in potential48.
## Nano Letters
Photoluminescence (PL) decay transient spectra of Cl-FAPbI3 films with a peak emission wavelength of $805~\mathrm{nm}$ was conducted to determine carrier lifetimes and quenching effects via transport layers. The transient PL spectra of Cl-FAPbI3 films with different quenching layers are presented in Fig.5a, from which time constant (τ) was obtained from exponential fitting. The PL decay of the neat Cl-FAPbI3 film on glass exhibits $\tau$ of $68{\pm}2$ ns, which is comparable to that of previous studies43,49. Transient PL decay of Cl-FAPbI3 on ITO nearly overlaps that of Cl-FAPbI3 on glass with only a small difference at the beginning, indicating a poor quenching effect of ITO to Cl-FAPbI3. It is worth noting that the result is contrary to that of FTO, since FTO is an effective quencher (seeing Fig. S10). As shown in Fig. 5a, the addition of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and SpiroOMeTAD as electron and hole quenching layers on Cl-FAPbI3 film can accelerate the PL decay, with observed $\tau$ of $14{\pm}2$ ns and $6{\pm}1$ ns, respectively. It has also been shown that free carriers can be generated in the Cl-FAPbI3 films at room temperature and subsequently transfer to the selective materials without assistance from a heterojunction50. As a result, the PL decay in the Cl-FAPbI3 film originates from the free carrier extraction or recombination. Thus ITO is not an effective quencher in device A and B, where the holes are extracted significantly more efficiently by spiro-OMeTAD. Considering the similar contact area between Cl-FAPbI3/ITO and ClFAPbI3/spiro-OMeTAD in full devices (both device A and B), the photogenerated positive carriers (holes) under illumination should be rapidly extracted to the external circuit, resulting in a large amount of electrons left behind due to slow extraction by ITO. This result also coincides with the interpretation to the oscillation and hysteresis in J-V curve indicating ITO/Cl-FAPbI3 heterojunction has a significant influence on device operation.
In order to further understand device operation mechanism, we conducted capacitance-voltage (C-V) measurements on devices at $1000~\mathrm{Hz}$ in the dark. Fig. 5b compares the capacitance for device B and C under various bias conditions. Under the same bias, the capacitance in device B is almost one order of magnitude larger than that in device C. A large capacitance indicates a large charge storage. Referring to the difference in device B and C, the large charge storage is caused by the Cl-FAPbI3/ITO heterojunction and charge carriers should presumably be stored in Cl-FAPbI3 close to the Cl-FAPbI3/ITO heterojunction, since ITO is a metallic material with a high conductivity. The large electron storage due to the large capacitance increases the electron quasi-Fermi level in the Cl-FAPbI3 which produces an energy level difference from the hole quasi-Fermi level in spiro-OMeTAD to form a photovoltage. A similar phenomenon is observed in device containing ITO/Cl-MAPbI3/spiro-OMeTAD (as seen in Fig. S11) indicating a same work mechanism. Fig. 5c gives a statistical relationship between capacitance in dark and $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ of the device B, showing that VOC increases with the capacitance. Furthermore, concentration gradient due to electron storage in Cl-FAPbI3 is proposed to form an electric field along ClFAPbI3/ITO heterojunction which provides the driving force for the charge transferring from ClFAPbI3 to ITO. In order to confirm the hypothesis, we fabricated a device based on a different architecture of ITO(glass)/Cl-FAPbI3/PCBM/Au. PCBM is phenyl-C61-butyric acid methyl ester, an n-type material that can rapidly extract electrons from perovskite19. Fig. S12 shows the I-V plot of this device, which achieved a PCE of $3.1\ \%$ while $\mathrm{V_{OC}}$ of $0.766\mathrm{~V~}$ was observed. This result contradicts traditional p-i-n devices since PCBM has large electron affinity and good electron transport property, opposite to spiro-OMeTAD. Similarly to the situation in device A and B, after the electron is extracted by PCBM a large amount of holes remain in Cl-FAPbI3 which allows the concentration gradient to drive holes from Cl-FAPbI3 to the ITO substrate. As a result, we can see that charge storage is important for device operation without an electron or hole selective layer.
## Nano Letters
As mentioned above, $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ formed within the Cl-FAPbI3 film via releasing the organic species is assumed to be responsible for device operation. Here, the role of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ in device performance was estimated by the relation between device PCE statistics and the amount of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . As shown in Fig. S13, the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ amount would increase as Cl-FAPbI3 annealing time increases, while the device performance reaches the optimal condition with Cl-FAPbI3 annealed for $40\ \mathrm{min}$ . As shown in Fig. S14, with an optimizing annealing procedure with higher temperature of $135^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , a comparable performance can be obtain with shorter annealing time. With prolonging the annealing time, a obvious degradation of Cl-FAPbI3 into $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ is also observed. Due to valence/conduction band of Cl-FAPbI3 localizing in bandgap of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , PbI2 formed at the surface can accelerate the charge stroged in Cl-FAPbI3 and inhabit the opposite charge in perovskite recombining with the charge on ITO51,52.
In a conclusion, we report a simple device architecture for flexible perovskite solar cells without HBL. An efficient performance with PCE of $12.70\%$ was achieved. This flexible device show great potential for large-scale production of solar cells via roll-to-roll manufacturing. With accurate measurement, it is assumed that the built-in potential across perovskite/ITO heterojunction is not essential for the perovskite device operation, while a large charge storage in the perovskite film is believed to ensure the photovoltage and provides the driving force for charge transfer across this interface. Meanwhile, the other heterojunction, for example ClFAPbI3/spiro-OMeTAD heterojunction in device A and B is important for device operation, as it can be replaced by other effective quenching layers. This result indicates that possibility for further simplification of device structures and that materials with high conductivity can be applied in perovskite based photovoltaic devices. Our work provides guidance for the development of large-scale roll-to-roll manufacturing of high performance perovskite solar cells.
Materials and Method.
Synthesis of FAI. FAI was prepared by mixing $3\mathrm{g}$ of formamidine acetate and $8.2\mathrm{g}$ HI aqueous solution (45 wt $\%$ in water) at $0^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $2\textrm{h}$ . The precipitate was collected by rotary thermal evaporation. The collected precipitate was dissolved in the hot mixture solvent of ethanol and diethyl ether to form a saturation solution and then keep the solution in fridge with $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\boldsymbol{3}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The white precipitate was collected by filtration and was dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under vacuum for 2 days.
And XRD profile of fresh FAI is presented in Figure S15.
Device Fabrication. The ITO(PET) ( South China Xiang Science & Technology Company Limited, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{R}}\sim8\Omega/$ ⃞, $T{\sim}78\%$ ) was treated with UV-ozone for $30\mathrm{min}$ then immediately transferred to a dry-air glove box. The $\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{2}}$ solution $(0.46\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ ) was spin-coated at $3000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for 30 s and then annealed at 343.15 K for $20~\mathrm{min}$ . After cooled to room temperature, the solution mixing FAI and MACl (50:5; FAI:MACl) was coated at $3000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for 30 s. Then the film was transferred out of the glove box and heated at $393.15\mathrm{~K~}$ in air for $40~\mathrm{min}$ . Afterwards the film was transferred back to the glove box immediately, where the spiro-OMeTAD was spin-coated with $3000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 30 s on the film. Finally, the gold was evaporated as counter electrode. The results of optimizing this process for devices with $\mathrm{ITO}_{\mathrm{(glass)}}$ ( Courtaulds Pefromance Films, R\~20Ω/⃞, $T\mathrm{\sim}92\%$ ) can be found in Fig. S16-S18.
## Nano Letters
Photovoltaic Characterization. A 1000 W xenon light source solar simulator (Oriel, model 9119) with AM 1.5G filter (Oriel, model 91192) was used to provide an irradiance of $100~\mathrm{{mW}}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at the surface of the solar cell. The current-voltage characteristics of the cell under these conditions were obtained by applying external potential bias to the cell and measuring the generated photocurrent with a Keithley model 2400 digital source meter. All the device was scanned by reverse direction with $20~\mathrm{mV}$ per step with a delay time of 0.001 s unless noted otherwise.
C-V analysis. The C-V plot was obtained from a Hewlett-Packard 4284A LCR meter. Electrical contact to the devices during LCR measurements was provided by a Janis cryogenic probe station.
